AB  - Estuaries, which typically have high turbidity and nutrient loads, are not ideal habitats for reef-building corals. However, in the context of global warming, turbid reefs have been viewed as potential coral refugia due to the shading effects and nutrient supplements. Nevertheless, this view was challenged by the fact that only a handful of coral species could survive under such turbid conditions. In 2011, we made an unexpected discovery of a coral community, composed of 17 coral genera, thriving on the dolos (wave-dissipating concrete block) of a harbor built on the estuary of the Agongdian River in southern Taiwan. This community has increased coral coverage from 28.8% in 2011 to 53.5% in 2022 and expanded to include > 100 species of scleractinian corals, spanning 1.6 km within the 1-4 m depth range of the dolos platform. Water analyses showed that aside from turbidity and suspended solids, the overall water quality in the harbor was comparable to the coral reefs of southern Taiwan. Turbidity appeared to limit the depth distribution of the coral community and influence the photophysiological performance of the corals for acclimatizing to lower light levels. Stable isotope data further suggested that the corals may benefit from heterotrophic feeding on the enriched suspended particles, compensating for the limited autotrophic nutrient supply from their algal symbiont. This discovery suggests that many coral species may be able to adapt and thrive in turbid waters through acclimatization. Such findings broaden our understanding of potential coral refugia in the era of global warming.
