AB  - The snake-star Astrobrachion constrictum (Farquhar) lives in a mutualistic relationship with the antipatharian black coral, Antipathes fiordensis. The behaviour of this euryalid with unbranched arms was studied in situ using SCUBA, over a 2.5 year period at sites within Doubtful Sound and Preservation Inlet, New Zealand. During the day the snake-stars generally remain tightly coiled around the branches of the host coral colony. Brittle-stars removed from host coral colonies rapidly moved across the substratum and climbed back into a living coral colony as soon as one was encountered. Transplanted snake-stars remained on the new host coral colony for the duration of the study. Observations on feeding behaviour of A. constrictum revealed that the snake-stars uncoil during the night and utilise two possible methods of food gathering. In the first method, two or three arms are held out rigidly in the water column to snare drifting plankters with the tube feet and arm spines. The second method involves the arms being wiped gently across the branches of the host cord colony, plankters and detritus being gathered and passed along the arms in this process. Mucus is also gathered from the coral polyps, which are not harmed. By using the second method, the snake-stars apparently assist in keeping the host coral colony free from epizoic organisms and from detritus which may possibly smother the colony. The diet of A. constrictum is generally catholic in nature, but comprises large amounts of detrital material and mucus from the host colony. (C) 1998 Elsevier Science B.V.
