AB  - The ability of 15 spp. of Atlantic reef corals [Stephanocoenia michelinii, Agaricia agaricites, A. lamarcki, Siderastrea sidera, Porites porites, Favia fragum, Diploria clivosa, D. strigosa, D. labyrinthiformis, Montastrea annularis, M. cavernosa, Meandrina meadrites, Dendrogyra cylindrus, Mussa angulosa, Mycetophyllia lamarckiana] to act as suspension feeders was demonstrated by their removal of suspended particles from sea water in culture vessels. Mean clearance rates varied from 16.6-145.5 ml water cleared/h per cm2 of live coral tissue. The lowest rate was found in P. porites which is primarily a tentacle feeder, and the highest in D. clivosa which acts as both a tentacle feeder and suspension feeder. Rates of particle clearance in A. agaricites, which is primarily a suspension feeder, were influenced by current velocity and type of food.
