AB  - Fan worms (Annelida: Sabellidae) are suspension-feeding polychaetes that rely on their specialized gill crowns to filter suspended particles or microscopic cells from the surrounding water to support their growth and reproduction. However, the mechanisms underlying their remarkable efficiency and flexibility in filtration remain elusive. In this combined experimental and theoretical investigation, we reveal that fan worms passively adapt to complex fluid environments by employing an adaptive bending response of their gill filaments. This mechanism modulates microscale vortices around the filaments, enhancing particle capture efficiency. Furthermore, the high compliance of the gill filaments not only allows for dynamic shape adjustment in response to flow conditions but also enables size-selective filtration of particles, achieving a balance between structural stability and filtration performance. This structural-flexibility-driven filtration strategy offers new insights into the filtering mechanism of marine filter feeders and provides inspiration for the design of bioinspired underwater microparticle collection devices.
