AB  - Global warming and human activities have substantially increased coral mucus release into the ocean. A portion of this mucus is transported to sediments via hydrodynamics, significantly altering sedimentary organic matter (SOM) dynamics, though the mechanisms remain poorly understood. Through integrated field surveys and laboratory incubations in a typical South China Sea coral reef (Yangmeikeng Sea), this study revealed how coral mucus regulates organic matter (OM) transport and storage in reef sediments. Field results showed that reef sediments had lower porewater dissolved organic carbon (DOC) concentrations (5767.5 +/- 186.3 mu mol L-1 vs. 7103.9 +/- 121.2 mu mol L-1) and lower SOM content (0.12 +/- 0.02 % vs. 1.42 +/- 0.20 %) compared to non-reef sediments, yet higher biological reactivity. Beyond sediment grain size, mucus-mediated regulation was a critical OM dynamics driver in the study area. A 130-day microbial degradation experiment showed that porewater DOC in experimental groups (EGs; mucus-amended) decreased by 67.1-71.0 % (from 7115.6 to 7998.3 mu mol L-1 to 2289.7-2415.3 mu mol L-1), compared to a 52.5 % reduction in the control group (CG). Correspondingly, sedimentary total organic carbon in the EGs declined by 17.2-21.7 % (from 0.86 % to 0.65-0.70 %), exceeding the reduction in the CG (14.6 %). Coral mucus-derived DOC was highly labile (>98 % degraded), with only 0.99-1.97 % persisting as refractory. Mucus degradation further stimulated ambient DOC and SOM remineralization, causing additional losses of 0.81-3.23 % and 2.43-6.97 % from their respective initial pools. The mechanism involved coral mucus acting as a microbially activated biogeochemical engine: its high bioavailability fuels microbial metabolism, accelerating native OM turnover via priming.
