AB  - Experimental evaluation of the sediment rejecting behavior in fungiid corals shows: sediments easily slide off from cupolate coral; some flat corals inflate the polyp to get rid of sediments; and, in most species, sediments are entangled in mucus and removed by ciliary action. Mucus production is limited by the number of functioning mucus cells. During continuous stress by weighting sediments, the tissue overlying the septal ridges gets cut and worn off. Damage instantly occurs in species with sharp septa, but relatively late in those with borad septa. Even minute tissue remains are capable of regenerating trophozoids attached to the old skeleton. Species which endure only low sedimentation rates are mainly confined to hared bottoms (e.g., reef slope).
