TI  - REGIONAL VARIATION AND PALEONTOLOGY OF 2 CORAL BEDS IN THE MIDDLE DEVONIAN HAMILTON GROUP OF WESTERN NEW-YORK
T2  - JOURNAL OF PALEONTOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - Rugose and tabulate corals occur associated with a diverse fauna of bryozoans, brachiopods and mollusks in 2 thin, laterally extensive zones in gray mudstone facies of the Windom Shade Member (Moscow Formation) of western New York. The lower coral-rich zone (Bay View Coral Bed) is characterized by localized concentrations of large coral including Cystiphylloides and Heliophyllum in Erie County, N.Y. and widespread occurrences farther east of the small corals Stereolasma and Amplexiphyllum in equivalent facies. The upper zone (Fall Brook Coral Bed) yields abundant corals mainly in the Genesee and Wyoming Valleys. Both units contain very diverse faunas (up to 95 genera per locality) dominated by filter feeding taxa and including abundant epizoans, indicating optional benthic conditions. Taphonomic data indicate very low rates of sedimentation leading to formation of shell pavements which were colonized by rugose corals. Both coral beds display similar environmentally controlled species gradients apparently influenced by turbidity and/or depth-related factors, and 1 bed (Bay View) displays local concentrations of corals which are mappable within outcrops. The coral beds occur near the southeast limit of widespread coral occurrence; the outcrop belt of the Windom is subparallel to the north edge of the Appalachian Basin, hence to depositional strike along the margin of a cratonic shelf to the north and west. Possible causes of widespread shelf colonization by large rugose corals include reduction of clastic sedimentation and/or shallowing of the sea. The apparent isochroneity of thin coral beds may be an artifact because the outcrop section parallels strike of the facies belts, which migrated during transgressive-regressive cycles. Windom coral-bearing units are comparable to other Middle Devonian coral beds and to coral-rich units in the Upper Pennsylvanian, suggesting a persistent community structure controlled by a particular set of environmental parameters.
