TI  - EFFICIENCY OF ORGANIC-MATTER ABSORPTION BY THE TROPICAL ECHINOID DIADEMA-ANTILLARUM PHILIPPI FED NON-MACROPHYTIC ALGAE
T2  - JOURNAL OF EXPERIMENTAL MARINE BIOLOGY AND ECOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - Absorption efficiencies measured by the indirect method for D. antillarum Philippi fed encrusting coralline algae ranged from 26.2-72.8%. Individuals fed filamentous epipelic algae on surface sediments, filamentous endolithic algae on coral dead heads, and coral rubble fragments had low or negative efficiencies. Encrusting coralline algae had the highest organic content on a dry-wt basis followed in decreasing order by endolithic algae on rubble, epipelic algae and endolithic algae on coral dead heads. The proportions of food types in the guts showed that small animals ingested far more encrusting coralline algae than larger ones. As these urchins grow, their diet shifts from encrusting coralline algae, compartively high in organic content, to epipelic and endolithic algae which are low in organic content. Urchins remaining in areas devoid of macrophytic algae may be supported nutritionally by encrusting coralline algae.
