TI  - PHOTOGRAPHIC OBSERVATIONS OF THE LIFE-STYLE OF A DEEP-SEA OPHIUROID ASTERONYX-LOVENI (ECHINODERMATA)
T2  - DEEP-SEA RESEARCH PART A-OCEANOGRAPHIC RESEARCH PAPERS
LA  - English
AB  - The life style of a deep-sea ophiuroid, Asteronyx loveni was studied from underwater photographs (7 stations) and trawled samples (11 stations) between about 800 and 1700 m along the Pacific coast of Japan in the Flores Sea, Indonesia. This ophiuroid typically clings to gorgonians (Radicipes ssp.) and pennatulids (Funiculina quadrangularis and Anthoptilum sp.). The stomach contents of A. loveni included fragments of crustaceans, polychaete setae, sediment particles and flocculent material, but no tissues of the host coelenterates. Bottom photographs show the ophiuroids extending several arms into the water column presumably for suspension feeding. There was rarely more than one ophiuroid per coelenterate host, perhaps due to intraspecific competition for perch sites. Stereoscopic analysis of photographs revealed that most of the ophiuroids perched 20-35 cm above the sea floor. This may be the most effective height for feeding on particulate material resuspended from the  sea floor by bottom currents.
